WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.140
If there's one thing
you know about sloths,

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.490
it's that they're,

00:00:04.490 --> 00:00:05.423
well,

00:00:05.423 --> 00:00:06.943
slow.

00:00:06.943 --> 00:00:09.540
But that doesn't mean they're boring.

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.320
In fact, sloths are one of the most

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:13.890
extreme animals on the planet,

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.973
and not just because they
poop only once a week.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.270
First of all, sloths
really are extremely slow.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:28.730
Their top speeds clock
in at just 0.25 kph,

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:32.070
making them the slowest
mammal on the planet.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.350
According to zoologist Becky Cliffe,

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:38.140
that's because, well,
sloths can't really see.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.957
Becky Cliffe: 60 million
years ago, they lost

00:00:39.957 --> 00:00:41.281
the ability to see.

00:00:41.281 --> 00:00:43.710
They went almost completely blind.

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:45.840
So, in the daytime, a sloth actually

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.040
can't see anything 'cause it's too bright.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.120
If you can't see where you're
going, you can't run around

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.250
because you're going to fall out the tree.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.120
Narrator: Moving slowly
may sound like a liability,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.900
especially if you're nearly blind,

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.510
but being sluggish actually

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:02.210
comes with all kinds of benefits.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:05.490
For one, they save an
enormous amount of energy.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:08.616
In fact, sloths use about 90%

00:01:08.616 --> 00:01:12.126
less energy than the average
mammal, which is vital,

00:01:12.126 --> 00:01:14.830
considering their diet
is a low-energy snack

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.310
consisting of leaves...with
a side of leaves.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.700
And as it turns out, being slow

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.060
also helps sloths avoid detection.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.262
You see, sloths are so slow that predators

00:01:26.262 --> 00:01:28.362
like jaguars and eagles,

00:01:28.362 --> 00:01:29.811
which use movement to hunt,

00:01:29.811 --> 00:01:31.490
can't even find them.

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:34.765
But some sloths take predator avoidance

00:01:34.765 --> 00:01:36.070
to the next level.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.850
Thanks to rare extra
vertebrae in its neck,

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.250
the three-fingered sloth can
turn its head 270 degrees,

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.540
which allows it to
smell incoming predators

00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:47.440
from almost any direction,

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.337
and that's especially
useful when you spend

00:01:49.337 --> 00:01:53.540
nearly all of your time
motionless in a tree.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.230
In fact, sloths sleep, mate,

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.093
and even give birth

00:01:58.093 --> 00:02:01.050
all while hanging upside down on a branch.

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.690
And, as you might expect,

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:06.100
that requires some pretty
extreme adaptations.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.640
Cliffe: They've got special
tendons in their hands,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.100
which lock into place.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:11.690
Narrator: And, along with their long,

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.390
hooklike fingernails,
allow them to hang easily.

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:16.820
Cliffe: They're like a giant coat hanger.

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:19.090
Narrator: And to combat a
rush of blood to the head,

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:22.140
which might be familiar if
you've ever hung upside down...

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.730
Cliffe: They have special sort of valves

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.110
in their circulatory
system that does actually

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.070
stop the pooling of blood in the head.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.753
Narrator: But if you think
that's extreme, consider this:

00:02:32.753 --> 00:02:37.400
Once a week, sloths risk their
lives on a daring journey

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.480
from the protective forest
canopy to the ground.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:42.178
Their mission?

00:02:42.178 --> 00:02:43.536
To poop.

00:02:43.536 --> 00:02:45.753
And it really is risky.

00:02:45.753 --> 00:02:50.040
Cliffe: We suspect about
60% of predator-based

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.420
sloth deaths happen while they're pooping.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.400
They literally risk their life
to come down to the ground.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.890
Narrator: So they have to make it count.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.090
And, boy, they really do.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.270
Sloths can lose an astonishing 30%

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.870
of their body weight each time they go.

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:08.810
Plus, they might get a mate out of it.

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:11.040
Cliffe: How do you find
a mate if you're solitary

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.920
and you can't move very far?

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:13.940
Well, they're gonna have to use

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.913
pheromones and scent markers.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.295
I think that coming down
and leaving a little pile

00:03:19.295 --> 00:03:21.725
at the base of your favorite trees

00:03:21.725 --> 00:03:23.890
is sort of like a little signpost.

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.450
Narrator: But if there's
one thing that really pushes

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:29.910
sloths over the top, it's their rough fur.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.593
Sloth fur has its very own ecosystem.

00:03:33.593 --> 00:03:35.776
Because they live in the rainforest,

00:03:35.776 --> 00:03:39.260
it's often wet,

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.360
which creates the perfect
environment for algae to grow

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.810
and provide sloths with green camouflage.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.480
But it isn't just algae that
calls a sloth's fur home.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.870
You can also find beetles, cockroaches,

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:54.780
and a species of moth
that's found nowhere else

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.340
in the world except for on a sloth.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:00.260
And the sloths don't seem to mind one bit.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:01.890
But that's not all.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.980
Their fur might actually save lives.

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.310
Fungi in their fur have been shown

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:08.900
to fight breast cancer cells,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.570
and they may also have
properties for fighting malaria.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.311
So, despite their slow pace,

00:04:15.311 --> 00:04:17.411
sloths are far from boring,

00:04:17.411 --> 00:04:21.050
and in some environments,
they aren't even slow.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.883
I mean, look at that daredevil go.

